WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.542 --> 00:00:09.214
Imagine if you live at the center
of a global conservation spotlight,

00:00:09.214 --> 00:00:11.758
but you have no voice.

00:00:12.175 --> 00:00:14.886
Your aspirations are not valid,

00:00:14.928 --> 00:00:17.347
and your knowledge does not count.

00:00:18.098 --> 00:00:22.602
Sadly, this happens very often in local
communities of key ecosystems

00:00:22.644 --> 00:00:24.145
around the world.

00:00:24.145 --> 00:00:26.356
In conservation, my field of work,

00:00:26.398 --> 00:00:30.819
I call it community-based
conservation washing,

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.572
which is basically
when conservation organizations

00:00:33.613 --> 00:00:36.575
claim to work for communities

00:00:36.575 --> 00:00:40.412
only to inform and impose
pre-designed conservation plans

00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:41.997
instead of including them,

00:00:42.038 --> 00:00:44.541
misguiding media and funding sources.

00:00:44.583 --> 00:00:48.003
Think of greenwashing,
and then apply it to conservation.

00:00:48.712 --> 00:00:52.132
Hopefully we can all agree
that approach is not right,

00:00:52.132 --> 00:00:53.925
and it needs to be tackled.

00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:55.969
And how about we start

00:00:55.969 --> 00:01:00.974
by twisting classic community-based
conservation questions upside down?

00:01:01.308 --> 00:01:04.477
Say, instead of wondering
how to bring science

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:06.646
and policy to communities,

00:01:06.646 --> 00:01:09.816
how about we find a way
to bring the voice

00:01:09.858 --> 00:01:14.988
and centenary knowledge of communities
to research and policy making?

00:01:15.655 --> 00:01:21.328
But see, the answer to such questions
is never a one-size-fits-all solution,

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:25.248
because it needs to be tailored
to the unique environmental,

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:29.210
social and economic realities
of each community.

00:01:29.794 --> 00:01:31.463
Let me give you some context.

00:01:31.504 --> 00:01:36.801
I get to work in one of the most
incredible wetland systems on Earth.

00:01:37.218 --> 00:01:41.765
The sources of the Kavango
Zambezi Transfrontier Conservation area,

00:01:41.806 --> 00:01:43.433
KAZA for short.

00:01:43.475 --> 00:01:46.603
But now KAZA spreads
through five countries

00:01:46.645 --> 00:01:49.981
and is home to iconic African wildlife,

00:01:50.023 --> 00:01:52.942
incredible landscapes,
like the Okavango Delta,

00:01:52.984 --> 00:01:56.529
and up to 2.5 million people.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:01.076
Its sources in eastern Angola
are the heart, the lungs

00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:04.162
and the backbone of the entire KAZA,

00:02:04.162 --> 00:02:05.872
yet they’re unprotected.

00:02:06.539 --> 00:02:09.209
In one of the many research trips
I've done to the area,

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:11.419
I met Mama Lerógio,

00:02:11.419 --> 00:02:13.880
the oldest lady of a Luchaze village,

00:02:13.922 --> 00:02:17.842
and she was genuinely surprised
to still see me alive.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.637
She told me the story of the Mukissi,

00:02:20.637 --> 00:02:24.432
this mythical, giant snake-like creature

00:02:24.432 --> 00:02:27.602
that protects the rivers and thus life.

00:02:28.103 --> 00:02:32.315
And she told me that if the Mukissi
spared me, my life,

00:02:32.357 --> 00:02:35.110
after spending four months
in dugout canoes

00:02:35.151 --> 00:02:38.238
from the sources to the Okavango
delta in Botswana,

00:02:38.279 --> 00:02:39.948
it meant it trusted me.

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:43.118
And if it did, so could they,
the Luchaze people.

00:02:43.702 --> 00:02:47.789
But see, most Luchaze children
do not know of the Mukissi

00:02:47.831 --> 00:02:49.708
or any other local tale.

00:02:50.083 --> 00:02:54.379
And that is because the 40-plus
year-long war they have endured

00:02:54.421 --> 00:02:57.132
has created a gap in storytelling

00:02:57.173 --> 00:03:00.635
and has stripped away ownership
of their heritage.

00:03:01.428 --> 00:03:04.347
So now the ancient wisdom,

00:03:04.389 --> 00:03:07.684
which is key to stewardship,

00:03:07.684 --> 00:03:11.604
is locked in the older,
disappearing generations.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:17.318
And so we started this journey
to bring back these stories

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.239
as an effort to restore
pride and ownership.

00:03:21.698 --> 00:03:26.453
Oral storytelling has always
played a fundamental role,

00:03:26.494 --> 00:03:30.331
not only in African culture
but in conservation efforts, too.

00:03:31.124 --> 00:03:33.126
We also do mapping exercises

00:03:33.168 --> 00:03:38.798
to help villagers visualize the influence
and impact not only of their land,

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:43.803
but also of their daily actions
beyond their geographic reach.

00:03:43.845 --> 00:03:46.014
To give them a sense of importance

00:03:46.014 --> 00:03:50.101
to counteract how they felt
abandoned all these years.

00:03:50.727 --> 00:03:54.522
And we also help give them back
the power of decision

00:03:54.564 --> 00:03:56.900
by instigating curiosity

00:03:56.941 --> 00:03:59.944
on alternative livelihoods
that they can trial on.

00:04:00.487 --> 00:04:04.991
All of this in an effort to bring back
ownership of their heritage

00:04:05.033 --> 00:04:08.161
and make them stewards of their own land

00:04:08.161 --> 00:04:10.371
and also of their own future.

00:04:11.581 --> 00:04:13.208
But see, through the years,

00:04:13.208 --> 00:04:16.461
that trust that I've mentioned
before, grew stronger.

00:04:16.503 --> 00:04:20.882
So now they scold me, they teach,
they feed, they protect me,

00:04:20.924 --> 00:04:23.510
they tell me secrets of their traditions.

00:04:23.551 --> 00:04:26.513
Children tease me and call me names,

00:04:26.554 --> 00:04:30.225
but I will never be one of them.

00:04:30.558 --> 00:04:34.646
And thus they will never
fully, truly trust me.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:38.191
But that is the point of this whole talk.

00:04:38.733 --> 00:04:41.653
They're not supposed to trust me, you

00:04:41.653 --> 00:04:46.282
or anyone else coming in
with a grand plan to save the world.

00:04:46.324 --> 00:04:48.910
They're supposed to trust themselves.

00:04:49.452 --> 00:04:51.955
Real community-based conservation

00:04:51.996 --> 00:04:56.960
goes much beyond ensuring an elder
has a ceremonial seat at the table

00:04:57.001 --> 00:04:58.336
and is interviewed.

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:03.466
It fosters fierce independence,
doesn't further dependency.

00:05:03.508 --> 00:05:06.970
Lasting conservation comes from within.

00:05:07.011 --> 00:05:10.598
From believing,
from belonging, from dreaming.

00:05:11.266 --> 00:05:13.893
So whether you are
a conservationist yourself

00:05:13.893 --> 00:05:15.728
or a donor or a media outlet,

00:05:15.728 --> 00:05:17.814
or maybe just plain curious,

00:05:17.856 --> 00:05:19.983
let's be vessels for that,

00:05:19.983 --> 00:05:22.318
for stewardship, for ownership,

00:05:22.318 --> 00:05:23.695
for pride.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:26.364
Don’t just tick the boxes.

00:05:26.406 --> 00:05:28.283
Ask the difficult questions.

00:05:28.324 --> 00:05:29.868
And always wonder:

00:05:29.868 --> 00:05:33.288
Are we working for communities

00:05:33.288 --> 00:05:35.248
or with communities?

00:05:35.832 --> 00:05:37.000
Thank you.

00:05:37.041 --> 00:05:39.919
(Applause)

